    <figure class="post__image post__image--center">
      <img loading="lazy" src="https://iainsm.github.io/biocycle_test/media/posts/10/valorization_concise.png" height="2550" width="4491" alt=""  sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" srcset="https://iainsm.github.io/biocycle_test/media/posts/10/responsive/valorization_concise-xs.png 300w ,https://iainsm.github.io/biocycle_test/media/posts/10/responsive/valorization_concise-sm.png 480w ,https://iainsm.github.io/biocycle_test/media/posts/10/responsive/valorization_concise-md.png 768w ,https://iainsm.github.io/biocycle_test/media/posts/10/responsive/valorization_concise-lg.png 1024w">
      <figcaption>Waste valorization allows us to work...</figcaption>
    </figure>

  <p>
    The Joshi Lab waste valorization project is an interwoven research cycle with the goal of producing new to nature industrially viable enzymes capable of degrading a variety of waste and contributing to microbial upcycling. We begin with in-silico candidate generation followed by directed evolution methods to probe the sequence/fitness landscape of these candidates and improve their viability. Industry-ready enzymes will be used for degradation of various recalcitrant polymers (e.g., plastic and lignocellulose) into more bio-accessible small molecules. These molecules, along with the enzymes themselves, are essential inputs for both existing and novel microbial upcycling systems that produce biomass, bioenergy or value-added chemicals invaluable to a variety of industries. As we make exciting developments in these three connected processes, the data we produce will be back-channeled to our machine learning model: training it in generating novel enzyme sequences, narrowing down candidates and making sequence-structure-function inferences for rational design modifications to make our enzymes as effective as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;<br>
  </p>

